From: davisd@nimitz.ee.washington.edu
Date: Fri, 15 Apr 94 17:59:36 -0700
Subject: [#94-4-115] Land vs Other Property 

> From: fnerd@smds.com (FutureNerd Steve Witham)
>
> My model is that property starts from forceful defense, and then gets 
> amplified a lot by social and other processes.  
> ..  Eventually you get to a point where force rarely has to be
> used...but I think the "effective defense" point of view is the best
> way to see which kinds of arrangements will work out long-term.

If "effective defense" includes the social processes, then I agree.
The mutual agreement I was talking about is not an actual face to face
discussion, but a case where each side sees it in his interest to
support the property system. The use of force becomes largely driven
by social sanction. When you and I disagree over a claimed property
right, one or the other of us in some part sanctions force. 

> The thing is, buying more guns doesn't change the effective balance of 
> power much between you and somebody else in a social situation.  
> Everybody else together still outgun you, and you're just going to make 
> them madder.  

And if everyone else disagrees with me, then I will of course go
along. We could also switch this situation around, and talk about a
single landowner protecting against the horde of landless marauders.
He would then be out of luck.

> So even though the whole thing's about force, it's not 
> *brute* force that wins.

Well, its not the brute force that I can personally deliver with my
body that counts. Nor is it the force which I can personally control.
It is the force which myself and others bring to bear that counts.

For example, the land owner, with few exceptions, brings very little
brute force to bear personally. He is supported by a social sanction,
a host of individuals who agree with him and support the few who
actually wield force on the landowner's behalf.

Yet the landless are not powerless in this regard. While it is true
that any particular fellow has little hope of overcoming the system
through force, the weight of their moral sanction, if withdrawn, would
make a big difference. People vote, they argue, they persuade, they
snicker, they write. These are the main powers used to keep the
current system in place, and they would be the main powers
overthrowing it. 

(Thats if people turn sensible, which I certainly do not expect. Just
keeping the theoretical nature of the discussion in view.)

Buy Buy -- Dan Davis      








