From: davisd@nimitz.ee.washington.edu
Date: Fri, 15 Apr 94 18:51:33 -0700
Subject: [#94-4-116] land property 

Steve continues

> Sure, although avoiding violence isn't a totally overriding concern.

Of course. You'll get no religious prohibitions from me.

> Er, do you mean symmetrical to my idea or to itself somehow?  I see
> you as adding asymmetry to asymmetry--that people who have land get 
> taxed by people who don't have land but do have other things the 
> landed people don't.  

When I said symmetrical, I was referring to a common starting ground
of no physical force sanctioned against others. You can't hit me, I
can't hit you. A claim to property is a claim to the right to use
physical force in certain situations.  Nothing wrong with such claims,
but I think it is the claimant of such a prerogative who bears the
burden of proof.

> I proposed a more symmetrical (to me) system
> where everybody is taxed for everything they own, but I don't think
> you've said much about that.

Sorry, I missed that part, and assumed you were simply opposed to my
land tax argument. Well, I may get to argue in favor of certain
property rights now. I don't agree to equally taxing all property. On
this point, I'll have to bear the burden of proof.

> >How about the prescription? When is it mutually
> > advantageous, and roughly the best that either of us can expect to do
> > in the negotiations, for us to agree with the other guy that he gets
> > a certain control?
> 
> This is tricky for me to answer, because my main answer is, "property
> happens."  

Well, "what will be, will be". Throughout this argument, I have never
maintained that my ideas are likely to come to pass, only that I
consider them the likely outcome of the interaction of reasonable
people. Certainly, "property happens". Your choice of dinner also
happens, and your choosing has a fair amount to do with it. On what
basis do you make choices on adding your sanction to claims to
property rights?

> The main large-scale
> change I'd make is to take away institutions that tax everyone and
> enforce the same (albeit slanted) rules on everyone.  

Umm, didn't you just come out for a tax on all property? Was that a
little joke? 

> Instead let 
> each person arrange and pay for the kind of defenses they want.

> That gets you as far as "having a market," where we should draw a
> dotted line "- - - - - - - - ".

Ah yes, well our little show may rapidly be drawing to an end. You
see, I'm not an anarchist, and don't find markets in defense
meaningful outside some context of rights. I hope it doesn't come to
"let the market decide what kind of property rights are involved". You
are part of the market. What do you decide?

Buy Buy -- Dan Davis      






